-- title:
--   Finiteness over ℤ of the Hecke algebra on cusp forms
-- statement:
--   Fix a level $N$ with $N \neq 0$ and a weight $k \in \mathbb{Z}$, and consider the space $\mathrm{CuspForm}(\Gamma_0(N), k)$ of cusp forms over $\mathbb{C}$. Assume [`CuspForm.HasIntegralStructure N k`](def/CuspForm_IntegralStructure.html#L6), which by its definition in the project says that the $\mathbb{C}$-span of the $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule [`CuspForm.intLattice N k`](def/CuspForm_IntegralStructure.html#L3) is the whole space, where [`CuspForm.intLattice N k`](def/CuspForm_IntegralStructure.html#L3) is the $\mathbb{Z}$-span of the set of cusp forms all of whose $q$-expansion coefficients [`ModularFormClass.qCoeff f n`](def/FLTPrelim_Modularity.html#L19) are rational integers. Assume also $1 \le k$, and let $S \subseteq \mathbb{N}$ be an arbitrary set. The conclusion is that [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra N k S`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18) is a finite (i.e. finitely generated) $\mathbb{Z}$-module. Here [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra N k S`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18) is the project's Hecke algebra: the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra `Algebra.adjoin` of $\mathrm{End}_{\mathbb{C}}(\mathrm{CuspForm}(\Gamma_0(N),k))$ generated by the set [`CuspForm.heckeGenerators N k S`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L14), consisting of the operators `heckeTLin k hℓ hℓN` for primes $\ell \nmid N$ with $\ell \notin S$ together with the operators `heckeULin k hqN` for primes $q \mid N$ with $q \notin S$. Thus $S$ is a set of excluded indices: the larger $S$ is, the fewer generators are taken (and indeed [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra_mono`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L45) records that the algebra is antitone in $S$). The statement is about finiteness as a module over $\mathbb{Z}$ only; no statement about a basis, rank, or commutativity is made here (commutativity is supplied separately by instances in the definition module).
--
--   This is the standard finiteness of the Hecke algebra $\mathbb{T}$ acting on cusp forms, as used in Deligne–Serre and in the Taylor–Wiles method; classically it is deduced from the existence of a Hecke-stable $\mathbb{Z}$-lattice of forms with integral $q$-expansions. The formal statement takes that lattice property as a hypothesis ([`CuspForm.HasIntegralStructure N k`](def/CuspForm_IntegralStructure.html#L6)) rather than proving it, holds for all weights $k \ge 1$ and for the algebra generated by only the operators outside an arbitrary excluded set $S$, and asserts only module-finiteness over $\mathbb{Z}$. It is the input to freeness of the Hecke algebra over $\mathbb{Z}$, to the dictionary between ring homomorphisms out of [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18) and normalised eigenforms, and to the residue-characteristic and newform-descent statements used later in the modularity-lifting argument.
